 Factor the equation as\[x(2000x^5+100x^4+10x^2+1)-2=0.\]The equation has a root close to 1; let this root be $a.$ Ket $u=2000(x/a)^5+100(x/a)^4+10(x/a)^2+1,$ so that\[2000x^5+100x^4+10x^2+1=u+2000(1-a)^5-10;$$2000(x/a)^5+100(x/a)^4+10(x/a)^2+1=u+2(2000-9a+1)(1-a)^5=0.$$Since $1 - a$ are positive roots of this equation, the value of $u$ is negative, so that\[x=a\left(\frac{-u+2(2000-9a+1)(1-a)^5}{2000}\right)^{1/5}.\]Now note that the coefficients of the polynomial are all positive except the constant term. If the equation has two real roots then a positive root is $\alpha$ and a positive root is $\beta,$ where |\beta|>|\alpha|, then the equation can be written as\[2000x^6+100x^5+10x^3+x-2=2000(x-\alpha)(x/\alpha-1)(x-\beta)(x/\beta-1)+2x+2-2(1-\alpha^5)(1-\beta^5)\]or\[\frac{2000(x-\alpha)(x/\alpha-1)(x-\beta)(x/\beta-1)+2x+2}{x}=\frac{2}{\alpha}-2(1-\alpha^5 )\frac{1-\beta^5}{\beta (2000+\alpha^3)}.\]Note that \[\lim\limits_{x\to 0}\frac{2000(x-\alpha)(x/\alpha-1)(x-\beta)(x/\beta-1)+2x+2}{x}=\frac{26}{11}.\]Also \(\frac{\beta}{\alpha}=\frac{2(1-\beta^5)(1-\alpha^5)-1}{2(1-\alpha^5)(1-\beta^5)-1},\) by equation (1).
$\boxed{\frac{26}{11}}$ is a positive root.
Denote \[f(x)*g(x):=\frac{2000(x-\alpha)(x/\alpha-1)(x-\beta)(x/\beta-1)+2x+2}{x}.\]Note that $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ satisfy the inequality \[2<\left(\frac{x}{\alpha}\right)^5<y<(y-1)^{24}\]for any \[0<x<\left(\frac{2}{\beta}\right)^{\frac{6}{2000}}=y<\alpha,\]
and \[\left(\left(\frac{\alpha}{x}\right)^5+\alpha-2\right)^5<\left(\frac{x}{\alpha}\right)^5+\left(\frac{x}{\alpha}\right)^4+1<\left(\left(\frac{\alpha}{x}\right)^5+\alpha-2\right)^{8}.\]Therefore $f(y)<0$ and $g(y)>0$ or $f(y)>0$ and $g(y)<0$ take place, hence the function has either two real roots or no real roots. Since we know that the roots exist, they must be one of a pair of real roots.
Expanding $f(x)*g(x)$ and equating constants we can show that the equation holds for any $x\in R$ if and only if\begin{align*}2(2000-9a+1)(1-a)^5+2(1-a)^5+\frac{3(26)-11(2)}{2}=0\\ \Rightarrow 27(26-\alpha^5)(1-\alpha^5)=11(26-2\alpha-\alpha^6).\end{align*}This leads to a third-order polynomial\begin{align*}&\alpha^6+\left(\frac{5559}{27}\right)\alpha^5+\left(\left(\frac{5559^2}{27}\right)-\left(\frac{621481}{*}\right)1^{12}\right)\alpha^1+2(26/\alpha-11)=0. \end{align*}Again, note the symmetry \[\left(\frac{621481}{*}\right)1^{12}=5559^{2}=27(5559)=27(26^2).\]Since roots can occur in a balanced pair, we know there are two real roots.
So the answer is $\boxed{\frac{26^2}{11^2}}$.}
